// src/tools/mkfs-pdxb/main.pdx -- R53.M1-004 (#1733)
//
// PDXB HOST-SIDE mkfs -- SIG-KEY-HASH EMBED SLICE (--sig-key + sig_key_hash stamp).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Scope
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Per design/tooling/volume-lifecycle-mechanism.md §9 M1 breakdown, this
// slice (R53.M1-004, #1733) lands two seam-functions the host-side
// `mkfs-pdxb` binary calls once per invocation:
//
//   main_parse_sig_key_arg(argc, argv) -> path_ptr
//       Scans argv for `--sig-key <path>`; returns a pointer to that
//       path (a caller-owned char*), or to the built-in default
//       (_main_sig_key_path_default) when the flag is absent or has
//       no value word after it.
//
//   main_sig_key_hash_apply(sb_block_ptr, sig_key_path_ptr) -> u64
//       Stamps the 32-byte sig_key_hash field at superblock offset 128
//       (PDXB_SB_SIG_KEY_HASH_OFFSET, superblock_fmt.pdx L133-134) with
//       the BLAKE3 fingerprint of the pub-key file at sig_key_path_ptr.
//       CURRENT STUB FORM: BLAKE3 primitive is not yet landed as a
//       paideia-as userspace stdlib (design/tooling/volume-lifecycle-
//       mechanism.md §3.1 "Cost" bullet flags this as the tightest
//       cross-repo dependency in R53). Until it lands, this function
//       (a) leaves the 32 bytes as zero, (b) emits a `SIG KEY HASH
//       STUB: ...` warning to stderr (Linux fd 2) via a direct
//       `sys_write` syscall, and (c) returns 0 (success) so the outer
//       `mkfs-pdxb` main continues to a completed superblock write --
//       the kernel-side probe accepts this under
//       PAIDEIA_DEV_UNSIGNED_ACCEPT=1 (§3.1 "Cost").
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Coordination with sibling slices
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Sibling issues in R53.M1 land complementary halves of the same
// tool. This file's discipline is to be self-contained:
//
//   #1730 (R53.M1-001) lands the outer `main(argc, argv)` entry point,
//     the file-writer (--file <path>, --size-mib <n>), the region
//     writer (delegating layout to R52.M2-003's mkfs_layout_compute),
//     and the argv dispatcher that invokes each --<flag>'s handler.
//     That dispatcher calls main_parse_sig_key_arg + main_sig_key_hash_apply
//     in sequence, from the same rax/rbx-saved region that also drives
//     --file / --size-mib / --uuid / --set-root-flag processing. This
//     file adds no such dispatcher of its own -- landing one would
//     collide directly with #1730's editing scope. Both functions here
//     are pure library primitives with SysV-ABI signatures the outer
//     main invokes verbatim.
//
//   #1732 (R53.M1-003) lands the dev signing keys at
//     tests/qemu/keys/pdxb-dev.mldsa65.{pub,priv} (verified present:
//     .pub is 1952 bytes as of 2026-08-23). This slice's default arg
//     value points at the .pub half; the .priv is used only when the
//     ML-DSA-65 sign intrinsic itself lands.
//
//   #1731 (R53.M1-002) lands tools/mkfs-pdxb.sh which builds and
//     invokes this binary. Not consumed here.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// superblock_fmt.pdx field confirmation (#1681)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// The 32-byte sig_key_hash field this slice stamps was frozen at
// R52.M1-001 (#1681, src/kernel/core/fs/pdxfs/superblock_fmt.pdx):
//
//   PDXB_SB_SIG_KEY_HASH_OFFSET : u64 = 128    // u8[32]
//   PDXB_SB_SIG_KEY_HASH_LEN    : u64 = 32
//
// No superblock_fmt.pdx edit is required for this issue -- the field
// exists at the expected offset and width. The offset/length constants
// are duplicated as _MAIN_SB_SIG_KEY_HASH_* below (following this
// codebase's cross-module-constants precedent per superblock.pdx's own
// header note that cross-module `pub let` references "have no
// precedent" -- values are re-declared locally).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Host-target ABI notes
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This is a Linux host binary (design/tooling/volume-lifecycle-
// mechanism.md §3.1 Path A). Linux x86_64 syscall ABI = the same
// SysV+SYSCALL shape paideia-as already uses for its userspace
// binaries (src/user/syscall_shim.pdx §3.2 template):
//
//   rax = syscall number       rdi/rsi/rdx = args 1..3
//   rcx (SysV) -> r10 (kernel side, arity>=4 only)
//   ret = rax; kernel clobbers r11 and rcx
//
// Linux syscall number 1 = write (matches the substrate's SYS_write).
// fd 2 = stderr. No arg shuffle needed (arity 3 < 4).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Failure taxonomy
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This slice defines no new taxonomy codes. `main_parse_sig_key_arg`
// cannot fail (it always returns a valid pointer -- either the arg
// value or the default). `main_sig_key_hash_apply` returns 0
// unconditionally in stub form -- the sig_key_hash bytes are known-
// zero after this function, and the stderr write is best-effort
// (a failed write does not fail the mkfs, matching the substrate's
// posture that this WHOLE feature is under a dev-only accept-unsigned
// gate today). When BLAKE3 lands, this function will grow real error
// codes in the sibling of R52.M2-003's MKFS_* band (0xFFFFED0E+
// remain unassigned).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Reserved-label discipline
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every label here is prefixed `main_parse_sig_key_arg_` (this
// kernel's reserved-label discipline forbids bare `if`/`loop`/...
// labels; the function-name prefix is unique across the whole link
// unit). `main_sig_key_hash_apply` is straight-line and needs none.

module Main = structure {

  // ============================================================================
  // Constants (mirror superblock_fmt.pdx offsets + Linux ABI numbers).
  // Duplicated per the codebase's cross-module-constants precedent
  // (superblock.pdx header note).
  // ============================================================================

  pub let _MAIN_SB_SIG_KEY_HASH_OFFSET : u64 = 128
  pub let _MAIN_SB_SIG_KEY_HASH_LEN    : u64 = 32

  pub let _MAIN_LINUX_STDERR_FD        : u64 = 2
  pub let _MAIN_LINUX_SYS_WRITE        : u64 = 1

  // ============================================================================
  // Storage.
  // ============================================================================

  // Default sig-key path (36 chars + trailing NUL = 37 bytes). Points at
  // the .pub half of the dev signing key pair landed at #1732. NUL-
  // terminated for POSIX open(2) compatibility so the outer main can
  // pass this pointer straight to sys_open when BLAKE3 lands.
  pub let _main_sig_key_path_default : [u8; 37] =
    "tests/qemu/keys/pdxb-dev.mldsa65.pub\0"

  // `--sig-key` flag name, NUL-terminated to 10 bytes for the byte-loop
  // strcmp inside main_parse_sig_key_arg. Length includes the trailing
  // NUL so the compare rejects both shorter tokens ("--sig") and
  // longer superset tokens ("--sig-keys") in the same 10-byte pass.
  pub let _main_sig_key_arg_name : [u8; 10] = "--sig-key\0"

  // The stub warning emitted when BLAKE3 is not landed. Exactly 75 bytes
  // (byte-counted: "SIG KEY HASH STUB" 17 + ": " 2 + "BLAKE3" 6 + " " 1 +
  // "primitive" 9 + " " 1 + "not" 3 + " " 1 + "landed" 6 + "; " 2 +
  // "sig_key_hash" 12 + " " 1 + "left" 4 + " " 1 + "all-zero" 8 +
  // "\n" 1 = 75). No trailing NUL: the write syscall is length-explicit
  // and a NUL byte would corrupt terminal logs downstream.
  pub let _main_sig_key_hash_stub_msg : [u8; 75] =
    "SIG KEY HASH STUB: BLAKE3 primitive not landed; sig_key_hash left all-zero\n"

  pub let _main_sig_key_hash_stub_msg_len : u64 = 75

  // ============================================================================
  // main_parse_sig_key_arg(argc: u64, argv: u64) -> u64
  //   Scans argv from index 1 for the token "--sig-key". On match,
  //   returns argv[i+1] (the next token, taken as the path). On no
  //   match, on "--sig-key" as the final token (no value word), or
  //   on argc==0/1 (loop bound-check fails immediately), returns
  //   &_main_sig_key_path_default.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let main_parse_sig_key_arg : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem} @{} =
    fn (argc: u64) (argv: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem },
      capabilities: {},
      justification: "R53.M1-004 (#1733). Pure leaf (no nested calls); no callee-save prologue -- SysV arg regs (rdi=argc, rsi=argv, immutable across the loop) and caller-save temps only (rax/rcx/rdx/r8/r9/r10). Body: (1) r10=1 (loop cursor; i=1 skips argv[0]=program name). (2) Loop top: cmp r10,rdi; jae default (i>=argc: fall through to default return). (3) Load argv[i] = *(argv + i*8) via `mov rcx,r10; shl rcx,3; add rcx,rsi; mov rcx,[rcx]` (proven idiom, src/user/dispatch.pdx L306 shl r8,3 pattern). (4) `lea r8, [rip + _main_sig_key_arg_name]`; `mov r9, 10` (byte count including terminator -- compare length settled by the module-header note above). (5) Byte-loop compare: cmp r9,0; je match (all 10 bytes equal, terminators inclusive). Load both bytes with the paideia-as #1248 mitigation pattern (`xor rax,rax; mov_b rax,[rcx]` -- same for rdx/r8, per src/user/string.pdx memcmp L55-58). cmp rax,rdx; jne next_arg. Else `add rcx,1; add r8,1; sub r9,1; jmp cmp`. (6) next_arg: r10+=1; jmp top. (7) match: r10+=1 (advance to value slot); cmp r10,rdi; jae default (--sig-key had no value word); else load argv[i+1] into rax and ret. (8) default: `lea rax, [rip + _main_sig_key_path_default]; ret`. No test instruction (paideia-as no-test discipline); mov_b via zero-extending full-register load pattern. Reserved labels: main_parse_sig_key_arg_.",
      block: {
        mov r10, 1;

      main_parse_sig_key_arg_top:
        cmp r10, rdi;
        jae main_parse_sig_key_arg_default;

        mov rcx, r10;
        shl rcx, 3;
        add rcx, rsi;
        mov rcx, [rcx];

        lea r8, [rip + _main_sig_key_arg_name];
        mov r9, 10;

      main_parse_sig_key_arg_cmp:
        cmp r9, 0;
        je  main_parse_sig_key_arg_match;
        xor rax, rax;
        mov_b rax, [rcx];
        xor rdx, rdx;
        mov_b rdx, [r8];
        cmp rax, rdx;
        jne main_parse_sig_key_arg_next;
        add rcx, 1;
        add r8, 1;
        sub r9, 1;
        jmp main_parse_sig_key_arg_cmp;

      main_parse_sig_key_arg_next:
        add r10, 1;
        jmp main_parse_sig_key_arg_top;

      main_parse_sig_key_arg_match:
        add r10, 1;
        cmp r10, rdi;
        jae main_parse_sig_key_arg_default;
        mov rcx, r10;
        shl rcx, 3;
        add rcx, rsi;
        mov rax, [rcx];
        ret;

      main_parse_sig_key_arg_default:
        lea rax, [rip + _main_sig_key_path_default];
        ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // main_sig_key_hash_apply(sb_block_ptr: u64, sig_key_path_ptr: u64) -> u64
  //   Stamps sb_block_ptr[128..160] with the sig-key hash. STUB FORM:
  //   BLAKE3 primitive not landed; writes 32 zero bytes and emits a
  //   `SIG KEY HASH STUB: ...` warning to stderr. Always returns 0.
  //   `sig_key_path_ptr` is currently unused; the seam is preserved so
  //   that when BLAKE3 lands, the same call site (in sibling #1730's
  //   outer main) becomes a real hasher without a signature change.
  // ============================================================================

  pub let main_sig_key_hash_apply : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{mem, sysreg} @{fs} =
    fn (sb_block_ptr: u64) (sig_key_path_ptr: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: { mem, sysreg },
      capabilities: { fs },
      justification: "R53.M1-004 (#1733). Leaf (no nested calls, only a direct Linux `syscall`); no callee-save prologue -- caller-save regs only. Body: (1) Zero the 32 bytes at sb_block_ptr+128 via 4x mov [rdi+off],rax where rax=0 (xor eax,eax once, then four qword stores at +128/+136/+144/+152). This matches PDXB_SB_SIG_KEY_HASH_OFFSET/LEN from superblock_fmt.pdx exactly; the field is qword-aligned so no mov_b or byte-loop needed. Doing the memory writes BEFORE the syscall marshalling matters because step (2) below overwrites rdi (the sb_block_ptr) with the stderr fd literal. (2) Emit the SIG KEY HASH STUB warning via Linux sys_write(fd=2, buf=&msg, len=75): rdi=2 (_MAIN_LINUX_STDERR_FD), `lea rsi, [rip + _main_sig_key_hash_stub_msg]`, rdx=75 (_main_sig_key_hash_stub_msg_len), rax=1 (_MAIN_LINUX_SYS_WRITE), syscall. Numeric literals used inline (not the `pub let` constants) since cross-module `pub let` references have no precedent per superblock.pdx L109-113 header note. The syscall's rax return is discarded -- stderr write is best-effort in stub form per module-header failure-taxonomy note. (3) xor eax,eax; ret (unconditional success). sig_key_path_ptr (rsi at entry) is unused in stub form -- documented via the module-header BLAKE3-not-landed note; when the primitive lands this function will open(rsi), read the pub-key bytes, blake3-hash, and stamp the 32-byte digest instead of zeros. No test instruction; no mov_b (all writes qword-aligned within the 32-byte field). Reserved labels: none needed (straight-line, single syscall).",
      block: {
        xor eax, eax;
        mov [rdi + 128], rax;
        mov [rdi + 136], rax;
        mov [rdi + 144], rax;
        mov [rdi + 152], rax;

        mov rdi, 2;
        lea rsi, [rip + _main_sig_key_hash_stub_msg];
        mov rdx, 75;
        mov rax, 1;
        syscall;

        xor eax, eax;
        ret
      }
    }
}
